tel 1.0

base exoskeleton
import soundness

info   command-line applications: arguments, flags, subcommands, tab completions, help and manpages from one body of code
detail
    _Exoskeleton_ is the frame around a command-line program. An entry point — `application`,
    or an object extending `Application` — hands the body a `Cli`: the arguments as a list of
    `Argument`s, the environment, the working directory, the standard streams and the login.
    The body recovers the structure of those arguments with typed declarations: a
    `Subcommand` is an extractor matched against an argument, a `Flag[Int](t"jobs")` reads an
    operand decoded to its type, a `Switch` reads only presence, and a `Setting` reads a value
    from the command line, an environment variable or a `Configurator` cascade, in that order.

    The unusual part is that everything else a command-line tool offers — tab completions
    for bash, zsh, fish and PowerShell, `--help` output, a manpage, and the table of exit
    statuses — is read off the same code rather than written beside it. Under
    `executives.completionsExecutive` the body is split into a pure prelude, which declares
    the flags and matches the subcommands, and an `execute` block, which does the work. A
    completion request runs the prelude alone, in a mode that records what the code asked for
    instead of acting on it, so a flag the program checks for is a flag it offers, under
    exactly the condition the code checks it. The `Help` tree is built the same way, by
    re-running the prelude at every subcommand prefix, and `roff` renders that tree as a
    manpage for `Manpages.install`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import backstops.genericErrorMessageBackstop
    import executives.directExecutive
    import interpreters.posixInterpreter
    import systems.javaBaseSystem

    val Build = Subcommand(t"build", e"compile the project")

    def mytool(args: List[Text]): Unit = application(args):
      arguments match
        case Build() :: _ =>
          val jobs: Optional[Int] = Flag[Int](t"jobs", aliases = List('j'))()
          Out.println(t"building with ${jobs.or(1)} jobs")
          Exit.Ok

        case _ =>
          Exit.Fail(1)
    ```

    Three contextual choices shape an entry point, each a given imported by name, since
    `import soundness.*` imports no givens:

    - an **executive** from `executives`: `directExecutive` runs the body once and expects an
      `Exit`; `completionsExecutive` expects an `Execution` and also answers completion,
      help and `{admin}` requests;
    - an **interpreter** from `interpreters`: `posixInterpreter` reads `--long` and `-s`
      flags, `posixClusteringInterpreter` additionally reads `-abc` as `-a -b -c`, and
      `simpleInterpreter` treats every argument as an operand;
    - a **backstop** from `backstops`, which turns an exception escaping the body into an
      exit status: `genericErrorMessageBackstop`, `exceptionMessageBackstop`,
      `stackTraceBackstop` or `silentBackstop`.

    The library is five source sets:

    - `exoskeleton.args` — the argument model and its typeclasses: `Cli`, `Argument`,
      `Commandline`, `Flag`, `Switch`, `Setting`, `Subcommand`, `CommandGroup`, `Status`,
      `Interpretable`, `Discoverable`, `Suggestible`, `Suggestion`, the `interpreters`
      package, and the `Effectful`/`Speculative` markers. Platform-neutral.
    - `exoskeleton.core` — the entry points and the executive model: `application`,
      `Application`, `Executive`, `Invocation`, `Entrypoint`, `Backstop` and the
      `backstops` and `executives` (`directExecutive`) packages, `Help` and `Install`.
    - `exoskeleton.completions` — JVM and Native only: `Completion`, `Completions` (the
      shell scripts and their installation), `execute`, `Execution`, `explain`, `helpTree`,
      `Pathname`, `pathnameDiscoverable`, `CliEvent` and `executives.completionsExecutive`.
    - `exoskeleton.manpage` — `Manual`, the `roff` extension on `Help`, and `Manpages`.
    - `exoskeleton.rig` — a test rig, not part of the umbrella's ordinary surface: `Enclave`
      packages an application as a daemon-backed executable and `Pane` drives a real shell
      in a pseudo-terminal or tmux session to assert on what tab completion shows.

    Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella, and the `backstops`, `executives`
    and `interpreters` choice packages merge with the umbrella's own. A `Cli` in scope also
    provides an `Environment`, a `WorkingDirectory` and a `Stdio`, so the body of an
    application needs no further imports to read a variable, resolve a path or print.

refer exoskeleton.application
refer exoskeleton.Application
refer exoskeleton.Cli
refer exoskeleton.Flag
refer exoskeleton.Subcommand
refer exoskeleton.execute
refer exoskeleton.Help
refer exoskeleton.executives
refer exoskeleton.interpreters

entry .application   wraps a program's entry point, giving its body a `Cli` and exiting with the status it returns
  detail
      `application(arguments)(block)` is the entry point of a command-line program. It
      builds an `Entrypoint` from the running process (its executable, found through
      `ProcessHandle`, and its script name), asks the `Executive` in scope for a `Cli` over
      the arguments, the JVM's environment and working directory, the file-descriptor
      `Stdio` and the process's `Login`, installs a handler for each of `signals` which
      dispatches to `Cli#trap` handlers, runs the block through the executive and then calls
      `System.exit` with the resulting `Exit`'s code. It never returns.

      It needs an `Executive`, an `Interpreter` and a `System` in scope, each imported by
      name. The block's type is `(erased permit: executive.Permit) ?=> Cli ?=> executive.Return`:
      under `executives.directExecutive` the permit is `Effectful` and the return an `Exit`,
      so a flag read anywhere in the block is its `Optional` value; under
      `executives.completionsExecutive` the permit is the inert `Speculative` and the return
      an `Execution`, so a flag read in the prelude is a `Prospective` handle, and the
      effects wait for `execute`.

      ```scala fuel
      alternative Function
        code
            def mytool(args: List[Text]): Unit = application(args):
              Out.println(t"Hello, world!")
              Exit.Ok
      alternative `Application`
        code
            object MyTool extends Application:
              def invoke(using Cli): Exit =
                Out.println(t"Hello, world!")
                Exit.Ok
      ##
      ```

      Packaged as an executable JAR whose `Main-Class` is `mytool` or `MyTool`, either runs
      from the shell. The status must be returned, not left implicit: `Exit.Ok` reports
      success and `Exit.Fail(1)` failure. An exception escaping the block reaches the
      `Backstop` the executive was built with.
  refer exoskeleton.Application
  refer exoskeleton.Executive
  refer exoskeleton.Cli
  refer exoskeleton.trap

entry .Application   an abstract class whose `main` converts the JVM's arguments and calls `invoke` under the direct executive
  detail
      The shortest complete program: an object extending `Application` supplies `main`,
      which converts the JVM's `Array[Text]` to a list and calls `application` with
      `executives.directExecutive`, `backstops.genericErrorMessageBackstop`,
      `interpreters.posixInterpreter` and `systems.javaBaseSystem` already chosen, so only
      `invoke` need be written. A program which wants a different executive, interpreter or
      backstop — in particular, tab completions — calls `application` itself with its own
      imports.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      object MyTool extends Application:
        def invoke(using Cli): Exit =
          Out.println(t"Hello, world!")
          Exit.Ok
      ```
  refer exoskeleton.application
  entry #invoke   the body of the program: runs with a `Cli` in scope and returns the exit status
      Abstract. Under the direct executive an `Effectful` is in scope throughout, so
      `Flag[T](…)()` is the flag's `Optional` value and `Switch(…)()` its presence, and
      `Out.println` prints at once.
  entry #main   the JVM entry point: converts the argument array and calls `application`

entry .Cli   the context of one command-line invocation: its arguments, environment, streams, login and completion state
  detail
      A `Cli` is a *capability* — exclusive, with the lifetime of the `Executive#process`
      scope that introduces it — carrying everything the body of an application may ask
      about the invocation it is handling. It is a `Console`, so `Out`, `Err` and `In` work
      through it; it provides a `WorkingDirectory`, an `Environment` and a `Stdio`, so
      `Environment[Text](t"HOME")`, path resolution and printing need no further givens; and
      it records what the code asks for — flags registered, requirements demanded, statuses
      admissible, suggestions offered — which is how an `Invocation` reports missing flags
      together and how a `Completion` turns the same code into suggestions.

      The two implementations are `Invocation`, for a real run, and `Completion`, for a
      tab-completion request; `helpTree` drives a `Completion` at every prefix. Most of the
      recording methods (`#register`, `#record`, `#present`, `#demand`, `#fault`,
      `#explain`, `#matched`, `#suggest`) are no-ops on the trait and overridden by one or
      both implementations; application code reaches them through `Flag`, `Subcommand`,
      `Argument#suggest` and `explain` rather than directly.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import interpreters.posixInterpreter

      def count(using cli: Cli): Int =
        cli.arguments.size

      def home(using cli: Cli): Optional[Text] =
        cli.environment.variable(t"HOME")
      ```

      The companion holds `Cli.arguments`, which turns text into positioned `Argument`s, and
      the `{admin} await` mailbox (`prepare`, `log`, `done`, `await`) used by the completions
      executive's administration subcommand.
  refer exoskeleton.Invocation
  refer exoskeleton.Completion
  refer exoskeleton.Argument
  refer exoskeleton.arguments
  entry .arguments   turns a list of text into `Argument`s, marking the focused one with a cursor
      `Cli.arguments(textArguments, focus, position, tab)` numbers the texts from `0` and
      wraps each as an `Argument` in `Format.Full`. When `focus` names an index, the list is
      padded with empty texts to reach it, and that argument carries a cursor at `position`,
      or at the end of its text when the shell gave no position (bash passes none), which is
      where whole-word prefix matching assumes it anyway; `tab` is the tab-press count the
      arguments record. With no focus — the ordinary invocation — no argument has a cursor.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Cli.arguments(List(t"build", t"--jobs", t"4")).map(_())
      Cli.arguments(List(t"build", t"--j"), focus = 1).map(_.cursor)
      ```
  entry .prepare   resets the `{admin} await` mailbox: clears the logged messages and arms a fresh trigger
      Called by the completions executive before it waits; the mailbox is global to the
      process, so one `await` is served at a time.
  entry .log   appends a message to the `{admin} await` mailbox
      An application running as a daemon may log diagnostic lines here; the next
      `{admin} await` invocation prints them when `done` fires.
  entry .done   releases a pending `{admin} await`
  entry .await   waits up to ten seconds for `done`, then returns the logged messages in order
      Needs a `Monitor` in scope. A timeout is not an error: whatever was logged is returned.
  entry #arguments   the arguments of this invocation, each an `Argument` with its position
      For a `Completion` these are the arguments after the command name, as the shell
      tokenized them; `Completion#fullArguments` keeps the raw `{completions}` request.
  entry #environment   the environment variables of the invocation
      Under a daemon this is the *client's* environment, not the resident JVM's, which is
      why `Completions.install` and `Manpages.install` take their `Environment` from the
      `Cli` rather than from `environments.javaBaseEnvironment`.
  entry #workingDirectory   the directory the command was run from
  entry #proceed   whether the application's own body should run at all
      `false` when the executive has already handled the invocation itself — the
      `{admin}` subcommands of the completions executive — and `true` for an ordinary run.
  entry #login   the user who ran the command: a `Login` with the username and, if known, the `UserId`
  entry #register   records that the code consulted a flag, with its operand's `Discoverable` and placeholder
      Every read of a `Flag` or `Setting` calls this, so a flag the program checks is a flag
      a `Completion` offers. A flag declared `secret = true` is consulted but never recorded.
      No-op on an `Invocation`.
  entry #record   records the exit statuses an `execute` block can return
      Called by `execute` with the `Status.Admissible` list reified from the block's result
      type, so a `Completion` and a `Help` probe learn the statuses without running the
      block.
  entry #present   records that a flag was specified on the command line
      A `Completion` uses this to stop offering a non-repeatable flag which is already there.
  entry #demand   records that a flag is required, and whether it was given
      Requirements are accrued, never failed fast: an `Invocation` collects the missing flags
      for `execute` to report together, and a `Completion` notes the requiredness for help
      while continuing to offer suggestions, since completing a command whose required
      flags are not yet all present is the common case.
  entry #fault   records that a flag was given but its operand could not be decoded, with the reason
      Like `#demand`, faults are accrued for `execute` to report together, and never
      preclude completions. `Flag#validate` is what calls it.
  entry #locate   the raw operand arguments of a flag, or `Unset` if the flag was not given at all
      Distinguishes a flag present with a malformed operand from one that is missing, which
      `Flag#validate` needs; defaults to `Unset` on the trait, and both implementations
      delegate to the `Interpreter`.
  entry #explain   attaches a paragraph of help to the current subcommand section
      The `update` function receives the prior explanation as a named context value
      (`prior`) and returns the new one; the top-level `explain` is the usual way to call it.
      Recorded only by a `Completion`, for zsh's completion header and for help.
  entry #matched   records that a `Subcommand` matched an argument during dispatch
  entry #matches   the subcommand names matched so far, as a contiguous run from the first argument
      On an `Invocation`, the recorded matches are deduplicated and cut at the first gap, so
      a match against a later argument alone cannot fabricate a false prefix; the result is
      the path `Help#local` takes to find the current subcommand's section.
  entry #trap   installs a signal handler for the rest of the invocation
      Takes a `PartialFunction[Signal, SignalResponse]`; handlers are consulted most recent
      first by `#dispatchSignal`, and one answering `SignalResponse.Defer` passes the signal
      on to the next. A handler matches a `Signal`, so a case reads
      `case Signal(Interrupt.Int, _, _, _) => …`. The top-level `trap` is the same call on the
      `Cli` in scope.
  entry #dispatchSignal   routes a signal through the installed handlers, most recent first
      Returns the first decided `SignalResponse`, or `SignalResponse.Reject` when no handler
      claims the signal. `application` wires each requested `UnixSignal` to this.
  entry #parameter   reads a flag's operand through the `Interpreter`, as an `Optional` of the operand type
      Abstract: `Invocation` and `Completion` both resolve it through the interpreter's
      `read`, which registers the flag as it goes. `Flag#apply` and friends are the typed
      front to this.
  entry #suggest   offers suggestions for an argument at the cursor
      No-op except on a `Completion`, where it records the candidates if the argument is the
      one being completed. `Argument#suggest` computes the prefix and suffix for the
      argument's format and calls this.

entry .Invocation   the `Cli` of a real run: a case class over the arguments, environment, streams and login
  detail
      `Invocation` is what the executives build for an ordinary run (as opposed to a
      `Completion` for a tab-completion request). It is itself a `Stdio`, exporting its
      `#stdio`'s `out`, `err`, `in` and `termcap`, and it accrues what the prelude asks for
      so that `execute` can check it before running the block: the flags demanded but
      missing (`#missingRequisites`) and the flags whose operands failed to decode
      (`#faults`).

      Tests build one directly to run an application body without a process:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import interpreters.posixInterpreter
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment

      val invocation: Invocation =
        Invocation
          ( Cli.arguments(List(t"--port", t"3")),
            summon[Environment],
            summon[WorkingDirectory],
            stdios.muteStdio,
            true,
            Login(t"tester", Unset) )

      given cli: Cli = invocation
      effectful(Flag[Int](t"port").value)
      ```

      It needs an `Interpreter` in scope when constructed, which it keeps.
  refer exoskeleton.Cli
  refer exoskeleton.Completion
  refer exoskeleton.execute
  entry #arguments   the arguments, each an `Argument`
  entry #environment   the environment of the invocation
  entry #workingDirectory   the working directory of the invocation
  entry #stdio   the standard streams
  entry #proceed   whether the application's body should run; `false` for an `{admin}` invocation
  entry #login   the user who ran the command
  entry #missingRequisites   the required flags which were not specified, in the order they were demanded
      Filled by `Flag#require` and `Flag#validate` in the prelude; `execute` prints them
      together and exits with status 2 instead of running its block.
  entry #faults   the validated flags whose operands failed to decode, with the reasons, in order
      Filled by `Flag#validate` in the prelude; `execute` reports them beside the missing
      flags.
  entry #parameter   reads a flag's operand through the interpreter

entry .Executive   the policy which decides what an entry point's body returns and how it is run
  detail
      An `Executive` is chosen by importing one of the `executives` givens by name. It
      builds the `Cli` for an invocation (`#invocation`), runs the body and converts its
      result to an `Exit` (`#process`), and may be able to build a `Help` tree from the body
      (`#help`). Its three type members fix the body's contract: `#Return` is what the body
      yields (`Exit` or `Execution`), `#Interface` the `Cli` it is given, and `#Permit` the
      erased capability the body receives — `Effectful` from an executive which runs the
      body only for real, `Speculative` from one which may also run it to discover
      completions.

      The two provided are `executives.directExecutive` and
      `executives.completionsExecutive`. A user-defined executive must declare all three
      type members; its `process` sees the body as `Cli ?=> Return`, not `Interface ?=>
      Return`, since adapting a `Cli ?=>` context function to an abstract interface type
      trips capture-root unification.
  refer exoskeleton.executives
  refer exoskeleton.application
  entry #Return   the type the entry point's body returns: `Exit` or `Execution`
  entry #Interface   the kind of `Cli` this executive hands the body
  entry #Permit   the erased capability the body receives: `Effectful` or `Speculative`
      With `Effectful`, `Flag#apply` resolves to the value and `Effectful`-demanding methods
      may be called anywhere in the body; with `Speculative`, they are available only inside
      `execute`.
  entry #invocation   builds the `Cli` for a run from the arguments, environment, directory, streams, entry point and login
      Needs an `Interpreter`. The completions executive inspects the arguments here to
      recognise a `{completions}` or `{admin}` request.
  entry #process   runs the body against a `Cli` and turns its result into an `Exit`
      Where exceptions escaping the body meet the `Backstop`.
  entry #help   builds a `Help` tree from the body by re-running its prelude, or `Unset` if this executive cannot
      Only the completions executive can, since its `Interface` is `Cli` and a `Completion`
      is a valid interface; the default returns `Unset`.

entry .Entrypoint   the executable behind an invocation: its path, its script name, and how to retire it
  detail
      `application` builds one from `ProcessHandle.current`; a daemon supplies its own.
      `Completions.install` uses `#script` as the command name the completion scripts
      complete and `#executable` to check the command on the `PATH` is this program. It is
      `Findable`, and is passed as a tracked capability (`Entrypoint^`).
  entry #script   the name the command is invoked by, which the completion scripts complete
  entry #executable   the path of the running executable
  entry #retire   asks the process serving this invocation to end once its work is done
      A daemon stops accepting invocations, lets those in flight finish, and exits; the
      default does nothing, for a process which ends with its invocation anyway. The
      completions executive's `{admin} shutdown` calls it.

entry .Backstop   what to do with an exception that escapes the body of an application
  detail
      A `Backstop` turns a `Throwable` into an `Exit`, with a `Stdio` to report on. The
      executives take one as a contextual parameter, so it is chosen by importing one of the
      `backstops` givens by name. `Backstop(report)` builds one which calls `report` with
      the error and then exits with status 1 for an `Exception` or 2 for anything more
      serious.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      given myBackstop: Backstop = Backstop: error =>
        Err.println(t"mytool: ${error.getMessage.nn.tt}")
      ```
  refer exoskeleton.backstops
  entry .apply   a backstop which reports with the given function, then exits 1 for an exception or 2 otherwise
  entry #handle   converts an escaped error into an exit status, reporting on the `Stdio` in scope

entry .backstops   the standard backstops, each a given to import by name
  detail
      An executive needs a `Backstop` to handle an exception escaping the application's body;
      `import soundness.*` brings none in, so one is imported by name. `Application` chooses
      `genericErrorMessageBackstop` itself.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import backstops.stackTraceBackstop
      ```

      All four exit with status 1 for an `Exception` and 2 for any other `Throwable`; they
      differ only in what they print to standard output first.
  entry .silentBackstop   prints nothing
  entry .exceptionMessageBackstop   prints the exception's own `toString`
  entry .stackTraceBackstop   prints the full stack trace, rendered through digression's `StackTrace`
  entry .genericErrorMessageBackstop   prints `An unexpected error occurred.`

entry .executives   the executives: how an entry point's body is run, each a given to import by name
  detail
      The executive is what determines the return type of an entry point's body and the
      capabilities available inside it, so it is chosen deliberately, by name, where the
      entry point is defined. `directExecutive` lives in `exoskeleton.core`;
      `completionsExecutive` in `exoskeleton.completions`, and both need a `Backstop` given.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import backstops.genericErrorMessageBackstop
      import executives.completionsExecutive
      import interpreters.posixInterpreter
      import systems.javaBaseSystem

      def mytool(args: List[Text]): Unit = application(args):
        val verbose = Switch(t"verbose", aliases = List('v'))()
        execute:
          if verbose.present then Out.println(t"verbose")
          Exit.Ok
      ```
  entry .directExecutive   runs the body once, for real, expecting an `Exit`
      `Return = Exit`, `Interface = Invocation`, `Permit = Effectful`: the body never runs
      speculatively, so a flag read anywhere in it is its `Optional` value, a `Switch` its
      presence, and `Effectful`-demanding methods may be called throughout. The `Cli` it
      builds is an `Invocation` with `proceed` false when the first argument is `{admin}`.
      Exceptions escaping the body go to the `Backstop`.
  entry .completionsExecutive   runs the prelude for completions, help and administration, and the whole body for a real run
      `Return = Execution`, `Interface = Cli`, `Permit = Speculative`. The arguments are
      inspected first: a `{completions} <shell> <focus> <position> <tty> -- <words…>`
      request (as the scripts `Completions.script` writes send it) builds a `Completion`
      and prints its suggestions in the shell's format; `{admin} pid`, `kill`, `shutdown`,
      `await` and `install` are answered without running the body; anything else is an
      `Invocation`, whose `Execution`'s exit status becomes the process's. Under it, output
      and effects belong inside `execute`: the prelude runs on every tab press.

      The given is an instance of `CompletionsExecutive`, typed
      `CompletionsExecutive^{parasite.threads}` because `{admin} await` supervises under the
      global threads; importing it is the same as for any other given.
  entry .CompletionsExecutive   the class behind `completionsExecutive`, for an executive built by hand
    detail
        `CompletionsExecutive()(using Backstop)` is the executive `completionsExecutive`
        returns, as a named class so it can declare `uses parasite.threads`. Its `help`
        delegates to `helpTree`.
    entry #Interface   `Cli`
    entry #Return   `Execution`
    entry #Permit   `Speculative`
    entry #invocation   recognises `{completions}` and `{admin}` requests, and builds an `Invocation` otherwise
    entry #process   prints a `Completion`'s suggestions, or runs an `Invocation` and takes its exit status
    entry #help   the `Help` tree of a body, built with `helpTree`

entry .trap   installs a signal handler on the `Cli` in scope
    `trap { case Signal(Interrupt.Int, _, _, _) => SignalResponse.Reject }` is
    `summon[Cli].trap(handler)`: see `Cli#trap`. Only the signals named in `application`'s
    `signals` list are delivered.

entry .Install   the error raised when writing something onto the host fails: completion scripts, manpages, a daemon
  detail
      Installing a completion script (`Completions.install`), a manpage
      (`Manpages.install`) or a daemon fails the same two ways, so the error belongs to the
      act rather than to any one installer. `Install.Error` is error 822, _the installation
      failed because `reason`_, with the reason's number as its subcode.
  entry .Error   error 822: the installation failed, because of the environment or an I/O failure
    detail
        Raised through a `Tactic[Install.Error]` by `Completions.install` and
        `Manpages.install`. `Reason.Environment` (1) means _it was not possible to get enough
        information about the install environment_ — a path, name or shell query failed;
        `Reason.Io` (2) means _an I/O error occurred when trying to write an installation
        file_.
    entry .Reason   why an installation failed: `Environment` or `Io`
      detail
          An enum with a `number` (1 for `Environment`, 2 for `Io`) and a `Communicable`
          given rendering each as its message.
      entry .communicable   renders a reason as its message
      entry #number   the subcode: 1 for `Environment`, 2 for `Io`
    entry #reason   the reason

entry .arguments   the arguments of the `Cli` in scope, as a list of `Argument`s
    `arguments` is `summon[Cli].arguments`: the words the shell passed after the command
    name, each an `Argument` which applies to its text and remembers its position. It is
    the usual scrutinee of a subcommand match:

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import backstops.genericErrorMessageBackstop
    import executives.directExecutive
    import interpreters.posixInterpreter
    import systems.javaBaseSystem

    def echo(args: List[Text]): Unit = application(args):
      arguments.each: argument =>
        Out.println(argument())
      Exit.Ok
    ```

entry .Argument   one word of the command line, with its position, its cursor and the piece of it being read
  detail
      An `Argument` is more than its text. It knows its `#position` (from `0` for the first
      word after the command), whether the cursor is in it and where (`#cursor`, set only
      for the word a completion request is completing), how many times tab has been pressed
      there (`#tab`), and its `#format`: which piece of the word the interpreter is reading,
      since `--jobs=4` is two pieces and `-abc` is three under the clustering interpreter.
      Applying an argument, `argument()`, gives the piece its format selects; `#value` is
      the whole word.

      `Argument.unapply` extracts the applied text, so `case Argument(t"--") => …` matches
      on it; `Subcommand` and `Pathname` are the extractors applications usually match
      arguments with.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val args: List[Argument] = Cli.arguments(List(t"build", t"--jobs=4"))
      args.map(_())
      args.map(_.position)
      ```
  refer exoskeleton.Cli
  refer exoskeleton.Subcommand
  refer exoskeleton.Suggestion
  entry .Format   which piece of a word the argument stands for
      `Full` is the whole word; `EqualityPrefix` and `EqualitySuffix` are the two sides of
      a `--flag=value` word; `CharFlag(index)` is one letter of a clustered short flag such as
      `-abc`; and `FlagSuffix` is the operand attached to a short flag, the `bc` of `-abc`
      under the non-clustering interpreter. Enum cases are not entries: `CharFlag` carries
      the letter's `Ordinal` within the cluster.
  entry .unapply   extracts the argument's applied text, so an `Argument` may be matched as `Argument(text)`
  entry .inspectable   renders an argument as `position: value / format => applied text`
  entry #position   the word's index on the command line, from `0` for the first word after the command
  entry #value   the whole word, regardless of format
  entry #cursor   the cursor's offset within the word, or `Unset` if the word is not being completed
  entry #tab   the number of times tab has been pressed at this position, if this is a completion
  entry #format   the piece of the word this argument reads: see `Argument.Format`
  entry #apply   the text of the piece the format selects
      `--jobs` for the `EqualityPrefix` of `--jobs=4`, `4` for its `EqualitySuffix`, `-b`
      for the second `CharFlag` of `-abc`, the whole word for `Full`.
  entry #prefix   the text before the cursor, or `Unset` if the word has no cursor
  entry #suffix   the text after the cursor, or `Unset` if the word has no cursor
  entry #contains   whether an offset within the word falls inside the piece this format selects
  entry #wrap   reshapes a suggestion so that it extends this word's piece rather than replacing the word
      For an `EqualitySuffix`, the suggestion gains the `--flag=` prefix; for a `CharFlag`,
      a flag suggestion keeps the letters already typed as a hidden prefix and inserts only
      the new letter, so choosing `-c` at `-ab` yields `-abc`, with `display` still naming
      the flag as `-c` and `incomplete` set so the shell does not end the word. A suggestion
      which is not a flag is left alone. `Completion#serialize` applies this to the focused
      word's candidates.
  entry #suggest   offers suggestions for this argument, if it is the one being completed
      `argument.suggest(update)` passes the prior suggestions to `update` as the named
      context value `prior` and records the result on the `Cli`, with the prefix and suffix
      this argument's format requires. `Subcommand#unapply` and `Pathname.unapply` call it;
      an application offering its own candidates for a positional argument does the same:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import interpreters.posixInterpreter

      def offer(argument: Argument)(using Cli): Unit =
        argument.suggest(Suggestion(t"debug") :: Suggestion(t"release") :: prior)
      ```
  entry #select   offers a list of options as this argument's candidates, and reads the one it names
      `argument.select(options)` needs the option type to be `Suggestible`; it offers every
      option's suggestion, marked as an operand value so the help tree does not descend into
      them, and returns the option whose text equals this argument's, or `Unset`.

entry .Commandline   the interpreted structure of the arguments: positionals, flags with their operands, and the focus
  detail
      The `Topic` of the two POSIX interpreters. `#positional` holds the words before the
      first flag, `#parameters` maps each flag word to the operand words which followed it,
      `#postpositional` holds everything after a bare `--`, and `#focus` is the flag the
      cursor belongs to in a completion request — the cursor's own word when that is a
      flag, otherwise the flag preceding it, so a flag's operand completes wherever the flag
      stands and not only when it is last.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val commandline: Commandline =
        interpreters.posixInterpreter.interpret(Cli.arguments(List(t"--tag", t"a", t"--tag", t"b")))

      commandline.locate(Flag(t"tag", repeatable = true)).let(_.map(_()))
      commandline.at(Flag(t"tag"))
      ```
  refer exoskeleton.Interpreter
  refer exoskeleton.interpreters
  entry #positional   the words before the first flag
  entry #parameters   each flag word, in its format, mapped to the operand words which followed it
  entry #postpositional   the words after a bare `--`, never read as flags
  entry #focus   the flag the cursor belongs to, if this is a completion request
  entry #at   a flag's operand arguments, or `Nil` if it is absent or has none
  entry #locate   a flag's operand arguments, or `Unset` if the flag was not given
      The operands of the flag's first occurrence, or, for a flag declared `repeatable`, of
      every occurrence in command-line order. Unlike `#at`, an absent flag (`Unset`) is
      distinguished from one present with no operands (`Nil`), which `Flag#validate` needs
      to tell a missing flag from a malformed one.
  entry #read   reads a flag's operands as a type, registering the flag on the `Cli` as it does
      Needs an `Interpretable` for the operand type and a `Discoverable` for its completions;
      the interpretation runs under `safely`, so a malformed operand reads as `Unset`.

entry .CommandGroup   a heading under which related subcommands are listed together in help
  detail
      `CommandGroup(name, description)` names a group; a `Subcommand` declared with `group =
      admin` is listed under the heading `Admin commands:` in `Help#teletype` and in its own
      subsection of the manpage's COMMANDS, with the description (a `Text` or styled
      `Teletype`) as a paragraph beneath. A flag borne identically by every member of a group
      is factored out and listed once as a common option.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val admin = CommandGroup(t"Admin commands", t"Commands for user administration.")
      val UserAdd = Subcommand(t"useradd", e"add a user account", group = admin)
      ```
  entry #name   the heading
  entry #description   an optional paragraph shown under the heading

entry .Discoverable   how a flag's operand type suggests its own values for completion
  detail
      A typeclass with one method, `#discover`, from the partially-typed operand and the
      tab-press count to a list of `Suggestion`s. A `Flag[Mode]` whose `Mode is
      Discoverable` offers those values after `--mode `; the given is taken from the flag's
      read site, and `Discoverable.noSuggestions` is the default when none is in scope. The
      companion derives one for any enum which is `Enumerable` and `Identifiable`, naming
      the cases in the style the imported `enumIdentification` given chooses.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class Mode(name: Text)

      given modeDiscoverable: Mode is Discoverable =
        (_, _) => List(Suggestion(t"debug"), Suggestion(t"release"))
      ```

      `pathnameDiscoverable`, in `exoskeleton.completions`, is the instance for `Path on
      Local`, completing filesystem paths a segment at a time; it is a top-level given and
      must be imported by name.
  refer exoskeleton.Interpretable
  refer exoskeleton.Suggestion
  refer exoskeleton.pathnameDiscoverable
  entry .noSuggestions   the instance which suggests nothing, the default for a flag read with none in scope
  entry .enumerable   suggests every case of an enum, by the name its `Identifiable` gives it
      In implicit scope for any `value: {Enumerable, Identifiable}`; which `Identifiable`
      applies is chosen by importing an `enumIdentification` given such as
      `kebabCaseIdentifiable`.
  entry #discover   the suggestions for an operand at the cursor
      `operand` is the text typed so far (empty when the word has not been started), so an
      instance may resolve it — descending into a directory being typed, as `Pathname` does
      — and `tab` is the number of times tab has been pressed at this position, so a repeated
      press may reveal more.

entry .Effectful   the erased capability which marks code running for real, as opposed to speculatively for completions
    `Effectful` is never instantiated: an `execute` block conjures it for its body, and
    `executives.directExecutive` grants it to the whole body as its `Permit`. `Flag#apply`,
    `Flag#require` and `Flag#validate` dispatch on its presence with `summonFrom`, resolving
    to a deferred handle (`Prospective`, `Requisite`) without it and to the value with it,
    and `Prospective#apply`, `Requisite#apply` and `Flag#value` demand it outright. A
    user-defined method may demand it too, to make an irreversible operation uncallable from
    the prelude that tab completion runs:

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    def deleteEverything()(using Effectful): Unit = ()
    ```

    `Completions.install` and `Manpages.install` demand it for exactly this reason; the
    `effectful` escape hatch conjures it outside `execute`.

entry .Speculative   the inert counterpart of `Effectful`: the permit of a body which may run only to discover completions
    `executives.completionsExecutive` grants `Speculative` as its `Permit`. It lets no
    effectful operation through: a flag read under it is a `Prospective`, and nothing that
    demands `Effectful` compiles outside `execute`.

entry .effectful   an escape hatch: runs a block with a conjured `Effectful`, outside any `execute`
    `effectful(block)` evaluates the block as though inside `execute`. Code which uses it
    forgoes the guarantees `execute` provides: in particular, a `Requisite` applied under a
    conjured `Effectful` may panic, since the missing-flag guard has not run. Its honest use
    is a test or an administrative path which installs something without an `execute`
    block, as `Completions.ensure` does.

entry .Flag   a named option, `--name` or `-n`, whose operand is read as a type
  detail
      `Flag[Int](t"jobs", aliases = List('j'))` declares a flag; applying it reads the flag
      from the `Cli` in scope. The declaration carries everything help and completions need:
      the `#name` (a `Text` for `--long`, a `Char` for `-s`), `#aliases` in either form,
      whether it is `#repeatable`, a `#description`, and whether it is `#secret` (read but
      never listed). The type parameter is the operand's type, which may be anything
      `Interpretable` — any type `Decodable in Text` is, through `Interpretable.decoder` —
      and defaults to `Text`.

      How a read resolves depends on the `Effectful` capability. With it in scope (anywhere
      under `executives.directExecutive`; inside `execute` under the completions executive)
      `flag()` is the `Optional` value, `flag.require()` the value or a thrown
      `Flag.Error`, and `flag.validate()` the value or a thrown `Flag.Error`, whose reason is
      `Missing` or `Invalid`. Without it — the prelude under the completions executive — the same
      calls return handles, a `Prospective` or a `Requisite`, resolved eagerly from the
      arguments, whose `present` and `absent` may be consulted at once but whose value is
      available only inside `execute`. Either way the read registers the flag, so a flag the
      program checks is a flag completion offers, under precisely the condition the code
      checks it.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label One flag
        code
            def tool(args: List[Text]): Unit =
              import executives.completionsExecutive
              application(args):
                val verbose = Switch(t"verbose", aliases = List('v'))()
                execute:
                  Out.println(t"verbose: ${verbose.present}")
                  Exit.Ok
      frame
        label A second flag, read only when the first is present
        code
            def tool(args: List[Text]): Unit =
              import executives.completionsExecutive
              application(args):
                val verbose = Switch(t"verbose", aliases = List('v'))()
                val level = if verbose.present then Flag[Int](t"level")() else Unset
                execute:
                  Out.println(t"level: ${level.let(_()).or(1)}")
                  Exit.Ok
      frame
        label A required flag, checked before the block runs
        code
            def tool(args: List[Text]): Unit =
              import executives.completionsExecutive
              application(args):
                val verbose = Switch(t"verbose", aliases = List('v'))()
                val level = if verbose.present then Flag[Int](t"level")() else Unset
                val home = Flag[Text](t"home", description = t"the home directory").require()
                execute:
                  Out.println(t"building ${home()} at level ${level.let(_()).or(1)}")
                  Exit.Ok
      ##
      ```

      After the first frame, `tool -<TAB>` offers `--verbose`; after the second, `--level`
      is offered only once `-v` is on the line, and `-v` no longer is; after the third,
      `--home` is offered always, is marked required in help, and a run without it prints
      _The following required options were not specified_ and exits with status 2 before
      the block runs.

      Two traps. `Flag#apply`, `require`, `validate` and `select` are `transparent inline`,
      so inside another `inline def` they are not expanded when that body is typed and their
      result keeps the declared union type: `present` on it then resolves against vacuous's
      `Optional` extension and answers `true` for every flag. Inline code should read a flag
      with `#present` (anywhere) or `#value` (with `Effectful`) instead. And a flag read for
      the first time inside `execute` is never seen by a completion, since the block does
      not run there: declare flags in the prelude.
  refer exoskeleton.Switch
  refer exoskeleton.Setting
  refer exoskeleton.Interpretable
  refer exoskeleton.Discoverable
  refer exoskeleton.Prospective
  refer exoskeleton.Requisite
  refer exoskeleton.execute
  entry .apply   declares a flag: `Flag[Int](t"jobs", repeatable, aliases, description, secret)`
      The type parameter is the operand type, `Text` by default (the `Defaulting` evidence
      is erased). `name` is a `Text` for a long flag or a `Char` for a short one; `aliases`
      may mix both; `repeatable = true` lets the flag appear more than once, and its
      operands then read together through a `List` topic; `secret = true` reads the flag
      without ever listing or completing it.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val jobs: Flag of Int = Flag[Int](t"jobs", aliases = List('j'), description = t"parallel jobs")
      val tags: Flag of List[Text] = Flag[List[Text]](t"tag", repeatable = true)
      val port: Flag of Optional[Int] = Flag[Optional[Int]](t"port")
      ```

      The `List[Text]` topic reads `--tag a --tag b` as `List(t"a", t"b")`; the
      `Optional[Int]` topic reads a malformed operand as `Unset` rather than raising, which
      is what a read in the pure prelude must do (`Interpretable.optionalInt`); a plain
      `Flag[Int]` reads through `validate()`.
  entry .serialize   a flag name as it is written: `--name` for a `Text`, `-n` for a `Char`
  entry .showable   shows a flag in its canonical form, `--name` or `-n`, as a usage message needs
  entry .inspectable   a debug rendering with the aliases, repeatability, secrecy and description
  entry #name   the canonical name: a `Text` for `--long`, a `Char` for `-s`
  entry #repeatable   whether the flag may appear more than once, its operands read together
  entry #aliases   the other names the flag answers to, long or short
  entry #description   the help text, shown by help, manpages and the shells which show descriptions
  entry #secret   whether the flag is read but never listed, completed or documented
  entry #apply   reads the flag: its `Optional` value with `Effectful` in scope, a `Prospective` handle without
      Registers the flag on the `Cli`, with the `Discoverable` in scope for its operand type
      (`Discoverable.noSuggestions` by default) and the operand name its `Interpretable`
      gives. Needs a `Cli`, an `Interpreter` and the topic's `Interpretable`; the
      interpretation runs under `safely`, so a malformed operand is `Unset` here (use
      `#validate` to report it). A `transparent inline` method: see the trap in `Flag`'s
      description.
  entry #require   reads the flag as required: the value with `Effectful`, a `Requisite` handle without
      In the prelude, a missing flag never fails fast: it is recorded with `Cli#demand`, and
      `execute` reports every missing requisite together, exits with status 2, and never
      runs its block, which is why `Requisite#apply` inside the block is unconditional. With
      `Effectful` in scope, a missing flag throws a `Missing` `Flag.Error` at once, for the
      backstop to report.
  entry #validate   reads the flag as required and checks its operand decodes, accruing or raising the failures
      As `#require`, and additionally interprets the operand with the topic's
      `Interpretable`, summoned at the call site over whatever `Tactic` its decoding may
      raise through, so no tactic is needed where it is called. In the prelude a flag which
      is present but malformed is recorded with `Cli#fault`, with the decoder's own message,
      or _a value is required but none was given_ / _the value is not valid_ when it gives
      none; `execute` prints them under _The following options were given invalid values_.
      With `Effectful`, a missing flag throws a `Missing` `Flag.Error` and a malformed one
      an `Invalid` `Flag.Error`. Unlike `#apply`, this is how a `Flag[Int]` whose decoder raises
      is read.
  entry #select   restricts the flag to a list of options, offering them as completions and reading the chosen one
      `flag.select(options)` needs the topic to be `Suggestible`; every option's suggestion
      is offered as an operand value, and the operand is read by matching its text against
      them. Resolves as `#apply` does.
  entry #present   whether the flag was given, read directly without the inline dispatch
      Registers the flag as `#apply` does. The reader to use inside an `inline def`, where
      `flag().present` is unreliable.
  entry #value   the flag's `Optional` value, read directly; needs `Effectful`
      Registers the flag as `#apply` does. The reader to use inside an `inline def` running
      under `Effectful`.
  entry #suggest   registers the flag on the `Cli` with the `Discoverable` in scope, without reading it
  entry #matches   whether an argument's applied text names this flag, by its name or an alias
      `--name` matches a `Text` name, `-n` a `Char` name; the argument's format decides which
      piece of the word is compared.
  entry .Error   raised by `#require` or `#validate` inside `execute`, where a flag's failure can no longer be accrued
    detail
        Thrown by `Flag#require` and `Flag#validate` only when `Effectful` is in scope — inside
        an `execute` block, or anywhere under `executives.directExecutive` — where the requisite
        guard has already run; it propagates to the executive's `Backstop`. In the prelude
        neither call fails: each records the fault on the `Cli`, and `execute` reports every
        missing or invalid flag together with exit status 2.
    entry #flag   the flag which was missing or invalid
    entry #reason   `Missing`, or `Invalid(detail)` with what the decoder reported
    entry .Reason   why the flag failed
      entry .Missing   the flag was required but absent: _the required flag `--name` was not specified_
      entry .Invalid   the flag's operand could not be decoded: _the value of `--name` is not valid: `detail`_

entry .Switch   a flag which takes no operand: declared like a `Flag`, read as its presence
  detail
      `Switch(name, repeatable, aliases, description, secret)` is `Flag[Unit](…)`, whose
      `Interpretable.unit` reads only presence, so help and completions show it without an
      operand. Applying it, `Switch(…)()`, yields `Optional[Unit]` under `Effectful` or a
      `Prospective[Unit]` without, either of which answers `present`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val verbose: Flag of Unit = Switch(t"verbose", aliases = List('v'), description = t"print detail")
      ```
  entry .apply   declares a switch, as `Flag.apply` does but with the `Unit` topic fixed

entry .Setting   a configurable option read from the command line, an environment variable or a `Configurator` cascade
  detail
      Many options should be settable not only on the command line but by an environment
      variable, a system property or a configuration file, with a fixed order of
      precedence. A `Setting` declares such an option once, by a canonical camelCase
      `#name`, and reading it consults, in order: its command-line `#flag`, derived as
      `--kebab-case` (so `logLevel` is `--log-level`); the environment `#variable` named
      explicitly, if any; then the `Configurator` in scope, whose standard cascade — system
      property `myapp.log.level`, then environment variable `MYAPP_LOG_LEVEL` — is activated
      by declaring a `Configurator.Prefix` given.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import interpreters.posixInterpreter
      import systems.javaBaseSystem

      given prefix: Configurator.Prefix = Configurator.Prefix(t"myapp")

      val LogLevel = Setting[Text](t"logLevel", description = t"logging verbosity")
      val Home = Setting[Text](t"home", variable = t"MYAPP_HOME")

      def level(using Cli): Text = LogLevel().or(t"info")
      ```

      A setting is single-valued, so its operand is read as `Text` and decoded by the same
      `Decodable in Text` as every other source. Reading one registers its flag for
      completion exactly as reading the flag directly would, so a setting must be read in
      the prelude to be discoverable. A user-defined `Configurator` given replaces the
      standard cascade; the command-line flag always comes first.
  refer exoskeleton.Flag
  entry .apply   declares a setting: `Setting[Int](t"port", description, aliases, variable, secret)`
      The type parameter is the value type, `Text` by default. `aliases` are the flag's
      extra names; `variable` names an environment variable consulted verbatim between the
      flag and the configurator cascade; `secret` hides the flag from help and completions.
  entry #name   the canonical camelCase name every source derives its own from
  entry #flag   the derived command-line flag, `--kebab-case`
  entry #variable   an environment variable consulted between the flag and the cascade, if named
  entry #apply   reads the setting through the cascade, as an `Optional` of its type
      Needs a `Cli`, an `Interpreter`, a `Configurator` and the type's `Decodable in Text`,
      plus an optional `Discoverable` for the flag's completions. Decoding runs under the
      configurator's tactic; an absent value in every source is `Unset`.

entry .Subcommand   a named mode of the program: an extractor matched against an argument, and a completion candidate
  detail
      `Subcommand(name, description, hidden, group)` declares a subcommand. It is an
      extractor, so the first argument is matched against it in an ordinary pattern, and
      matching has two effects beyond the test: it offers the subcommand as a suggestion
      for that argument (adding to whatever other extractors offered at the same position),
      and on a match it records the name with `Cli#matched`. A description written with
      `e"…"` carries styling for the shells which show it; `hidden = true` keeps the
      subcommand out of help and of every shell's listing (zsh still completes it when
      typed); `group` places it under a `CommandGroup` heading.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import backstops.genericErrorMessageBackstop
      import executives.directExecutive
      import interpreters.posixInterpreter
      import systems.javaBaseSystem

      val Build = Subcommand(t"build", e"compile the project")
      val Test = Subcommand(t"test", e"run the tests")

      def tool(args: List[Text]): Unit = application(args):
        arguments match
          case Build() :: _ => Out.println(t"building");  Exit.Ok
          case Test() :: _  => Out.println(t"testing");   Exit.Ok
          case _            => Out.println(t"unknown command"); Exit.Fail(1)
      ```

      Subcommands nest by matching again on the remaining arguments, and the help tree
      follows the same nesting. A name may contain a space (`red hat`), which the shells
      quote.
  refer exoskeleton.CommandGroup
  refer exoskeleton.Argument
  refer exoskeleton.Help
  entry #name   the word which selects the subcommand
  entry #description   the help text, a `Text` or a styled `Teletype`
  entry #hidden   whether the subcommand is kept out of help and listings
  entry #group   the `CommandGroup` it is listed under, if any
  entry #unapply   matches an argument by name, offering the subcommand as a suggestion and recording the match
      Needs a `Cli`. The suggestion is offered whether or not the argument matches, which
      is what makes every arm of a `match` a candidate for completion.

entry .Status   a meaningful exit status: a code with a description, declared as an object and returned from `execute`
  detail
      An exit code on its own says little, so a failure the program anticipates is declared
      as a `Status`, which is a `Termination` and may be returned wherever an `Exit` is:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      object CannotConnect extends Status(1, t"the server could not be reached")
      object BadConfig extends Status(2, t"the configuration file was invalid")
      ```

      Returning one from an `execute` block both sets the process's exit code and makes the
      status discoverable: the block's result type — kept precise by `execute`'s `Precise`
      bound — is the union of the singleton types of every status it can return, which
      `Status.Admissible` reifies as the list of their values, so help and the manpage list
      them under _Exit statuses_ without the application naming them twice.

      A status must be a top-level `object`, not a `val` or a local object: capture checking
      rejects the singleton type of a `val` in the union, and the `Admissible` macro reports
      a non-singleton member as an error rather than letting statuses silently vanish from
      the documentation.
  refer exoskeleton.execute
  refer exoskeleton.Help
  entry .Admissible   the typeclass which reifies the statuses in an `execute` block's result type
    detail
        `result is Status.Admissible` is derived by a macro for any `result`: the type is
        dealiased, its union decomposed, and each member which is a singleton `Status` is
        referred to again as a value; `Nothing` and any other `Termination` (`Exit` and its
        cases) contribute nothing. A `Status` member which is not a singleton is a compile
        error naming it, and a member which is not a `Termination` at all is another.
    entry .derived   the `transparent inline given` which derives the instance for any result type
    entry #Self   the result type
    entry #statuses   the statuses the type admits, as values
  entry #code   the exit code
  entry #description   what the status means, shown in help and manpages
  entry #exit   the status as an `Exit`: `Exit(code)`

entry .Prospective   the handle for a flag read in the prelude: its presence may be consulted now, its value only inside `execute`
  detail
      `Flag#apply` returns a `Prospective[Topic]` when no `Effectful` is in scope.
      Resolution is eager — the `#value` was read from the arguments when the handle was
      made, so it captures no `Cli` — and `#present` and `#absent` may be consulted freely
      in the prelude, to decide what else to offer. Applying the handle, `handle()`, demands
      the `Effectful` which only an `execute` block provides.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import backstops.genericErrorMessageBackstop
      import executives.completionsExecutive
      import interpreters.posixInterpreter
      import systems.javaBaseSystem

      def tool(args: List[Text]): Unit = application(args):
        val jobs: Prospective[Int] = Flag[Int](t"jobs")()
        execute:
          Out.println(t"jobs: ${jobs().or(1)}")
          Exit.Ok
      ```
  refer exoskeleton.Flag
  refer exoskeleton.Requisite
  entry #flag   the flag this handle was read from
  entry #value   the value as read, an `Optional`; consultable in the prelude
  entry #present   whether the flag was given
  entry #absent   whether the flag was not given
  entry #apply   the `Optional` value; needs `Effectful`

entry .Requisite   the handle for a required flag read in the prelude: unconditionally a value inside `execute`
  detail
      `Flag#require` and `Flag#validate` return a `Requisite[Topic]` when no `Effectful` is
      in scope. Creating it records the requirement on the `Cli`; if the flag is missing or
      malformed, `execute` reports every such flag in one message and never runs its block,
      so applying the handle inside the block yields the value itself. As with
      `Prospective`, `#value`, `#present` and `#absent` may be consulted in the prelude.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import backstops.genericErrorMessageBackstop
      import executives.completionsExecutive
      import interpreters.posixInterpreter
      import systems.javaBaseSystem

      def tool(args: List[Text]): Unit = application(args):
        val home: Requisite[Text] = Flag[Text](t"home").require()
        val jobs: Requisite[Int] = Flag[Int](t"jobs").validate()
        execute:
          Out.println(t"building ${home()} with ${jobs()} jobs")
          Exit.Ok
      ```
  refer exoskeleton.Flag
  refer exoskeleton.Prospective
  entry #flag   the flag this handle was read from
  entry #value   the value as read, an `Optional`; consultable in the prelude
  entry #present   whether the flag was given
  entry #absent   whether the flag was not given
  entry #apply   the value itself; needs `Effectful`
      Infallible only because `execute`'s guard has already aborted when the flag is
      missing; under a conjured `Effectful` (the `effectful` escape hatch) that guarantee
      is void and a missing flag panics.

entry .Interpretable   how a flag's operand arguments are read as a type
  detail
      A typeclass over the operand type: `#interpret` takes the flag's operand arguments
      (every occurrence's, for a repeatable flag) and yields the value, or `Unset`. It also
      says whether the type takes an operand at all (`#operand`, false only for `Unit`) and
      what to call it in help (`#placeholder`, the `file` of `--log <file>`). Any type
      `Decodable in Text` is interpretable through `.decoder`, which decodes the first
      operand; a type with its own notion of an operand writes an instance by hand, a
      lambda from the arguments sufficing:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class Mode(name: Text)

      given modeInterpretable: Mode is Interpretable =
        case argument :: _ => Mode(argument())
        case _             => Unset
      ```

      Purity matters in the prelude: `Flag#apply` runs the instance under `safely`, so an
      instance which may raise (a `Decodable` whose decoding raises `Number.Error`, say)
      reads a malformed operand as `Unset` there and is reported properly only through
      `Flag#validate`, which summons the instance over its tactic. The `Optional[…]`
      instances (`.optionalInt` and `.optional`) are the pure reading for such a type.
  refer exoskeleton.Flag
  refer exoskeleton.Discoverable
  entry .unit   the instance for `Unit`: no operand, presence alone
      What a `Switch` reads through; `operand` is false, so help shows no placeholder.
  entry .decoder   the instance for any type `Decodable in Text`: the first operand, decoded
      In implicit scope. A repeatable `Flag[Text]` still reads its first operand through
      this; a `List` topic reads them all.
  entry .optionalInt   the pure instance for `Optional[Int]`: a malformed operand is `Unset`, nothing raises
      An `inline given`; the decoder is summoned at the expansion site over the tactic it
      may raise through and run under `safely`. `Optional[…]` is a union, which no type
      parameter can be inferred through, so each such instance is made separately.
  entry .optionalLong   the pure instance for `Optional[Long]`
  entry .optionalDouble   the pure instance for `Optional[Double]`
  entry .optional   makes the pure `Optional[operand]` instance for any `operand: Decodable in Text`
      `given Optional[Port] is Interpretable = Interpretable.optional[Port]` declares one for
      another type.
  entry .list   the instance for `List[element]`: every operand interpreted as an element
      `Flag[List[Text]](t"tag", repeatable = true)()` reads `--tag a --tag b` as `List(t"a",
      t"b")`; one malformed operand makes the whole `Unset`. As pure as its element's
      instance, and with the element's placeholder.
  entry #operand   whether the type takes an operand; `true` by default
  entry #placeholder   the name to show for the operand in help, or `Unset` for the generic `value`
  entry #operandName   the placeholder, or `value`, or `Unset` when the type takes no operand
  entry #interpret   the value read from the operand arguments, or `Unset`
  entry #map   an instance for another type, by mapping the interpreted value
      `Interpretable[Text].map(Mode(_))` keeps the operand flag and placeholder of the
      original.

entry .Interpreter   the policy which turns the flat argument list into flags, operands and positionals
  detail
      The interpreter in scope is what turns *arguments* (the texts the shell hands over)
      into *parameters* (what they mean). It is chosen by importing one of the
      `interpreters` givens by name, since `-rA4` is three flags and an operand under one
      convention and one flag with the operand `rA4` under another. An `Interpreter`
      interprets the arguments once into its `#Topic` (a `Commandline` for the POSIX
      interpreters), and then answers queries about flags against that.
  refer exoskeleton.interpreters
  refer exoskeleton.Commandline
  entry #Topic   the interpreted form of the arguments
  entry #interpret   interprets the arguments into the topic
  entry #focus   the flag the cursor belongs to in a completion request, if any
  entry #find   a flag's operand arguments, or `Nil`
  entry #locate   a flag's operand arguments, or `Unset` if the flag was not given
      Defaulted to `Unset` for interpreters which cannot make the distinction; the POSIX
      interpreters override it, which is what lets `Flag#validate` tell a missing flag from
      a malformed one.
  entry #read   reads a flag's operands as a type, registering the flag on the `Cli`

entry .interpreters   the standard interpreters, each a given to import by name
  detail
      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import interpreters.posixInterpreter
      ```

      Both POSIX interpreters read `--long`, `-s`, `--long=value` and `--long value`,
      treat everything after a bare `--` as postpositional, and take the operands of a
      flag to be the words up to the next flag; they differ only in how a short flag longer
      than one letter is read.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val args: List[Argument] = Cli.arguments(List(t"-abc", t"x"))

      interpreters.posixClusteringInterpreter.interpret(args).at(Flag('c')).map(_())
      interpreters.posixInterpreter.interpret(args).at(Flag('a')).map(_())
      ```
  entry .posixInterpreter   `--long` and `-s` flags; `-abc` is the flag `-a` with the operand `bc`
      The interpreter `Application` chooses. A short flag with text attached, as in `-j4`,
      reads the text as its operand.
  entry .posixClusteringInterpreter   as `posixInterpreter`, but `-abc` is `-a -b -c`, with the last letter taking the operand
      A two-character short flag is unchanged; a long flag is never treated as a cluster.
      Completing inside a cluster extends it, so choosing `-c` at `-ab` gives `-abc`.
  entry .simpleInterpreter   every argument is an operand: no flags are read, and every flag reads as `Unset`
      For a program which handles its words itself; `focus` and `locate` give nothing, so
      no flag completes.

entry .Login   the user who ran the command: the username and, if known, the platform's user id
  detail
      `application` fills it from `ProcessHandle.current().info().user()`, with no id; a
      daemon's launcher may supply the id. Tests construct one directly, `Login(t"tester",
      Unset)`.
  entry #username   the user's name
  entry #id   the `UserId`, or `Unset` when the launcher did not report one

entry .UserId   the platform's identifier for a user: a numeric uid on Unix, a SID on Windows, carried as text
  detail
      The two forms share no numeric representation, so a `UserId` is an opaque `Text`,
      and `#unix` and `#sid` read whichever applies.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      UserId(t"501").unix
      UserId(t"S-1-5-21-1").sid
      ```
  entry .apply   wraps a user id's text
  entry .showable   shows the id as its text
  entry .encodable   encodes the id as its text
  entry .decodable   decodes any text as an id
  entry .text   the id as text
  entry .unix   the numeric uid, or `Unset` if the text is not a number
  entry .sid   the Windows security identifier, or `Unset` if the text does not begin with `S-`

entry .Shell   the shells completions are generated for: `Zsh`, `Bash`, `Fish` and `Powershell`
  detail
      An enum, with the cases as completion targets: `Completions.script(shell, command)`
      writes the script for each, `Completion#serialize` formats suggestions as each expects,
      and in the rig `shell.pane()` runs one. It decodes from and encodes to its lowercase
      name.
  entry .decoder   reads a shell from its name, case-insensitively
  entry .encodable   encodes a shell as its lowercase name
  entry .showable   shows a shell as its lowercase name

entry .Suggestible   how a value offers itself as a completion candidate
  detail
      A typeclass with one method, `#suggest`, from a value to a `Suggestion`.
      `Argument#select` and `Flag#select` take a list of `Suggestible` options, offer each
      one's suggestion and read the chosen one back by its text.
  entry #suggest   the suggestion for a value

entry .Suggestion   one completion candidate: the text to insert, with a description and how the shell should treat it
  detail
      A `Suggestion` is what every completion source — a `Subcommand`, a `Discoverable`, a
      `Pathname`, a `Flag` listing — produces, and what `Completion#serialize` renders for
      the shell. `#core` is the text inserted, between `#prefix` and `#suffix` (so `#text`
      is the whole word); `#description` is shown by zsh, fish and PowerShell; `#hidden`
      candidates are matched but not listed (and only zsh matches them); `#incomplete`
      tells the shell not to end the word, for a path completed a segment at a time;
      `#aliases` list the other names shown beside it; `#operand` marks a value offered for
      an operand rather than a subcommand, so the help tree does not descend into it; and
      `#display` is what the menu shows when it differs from what is inserted.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Suggestion(t"release", t"an optimised build")
      Suggestion(t"src/", incomplete = true)
      ```
  refer exoskeleton.Discoverable
  refer exoskeleton.Completion
  entry .apply   makes a suggestion, with every field but `core` defaulted
  entry #core   the text inserted
  entry #description   the description shown beside it, a `Text` or a styled `Teletype`, if any
  entry #hidden   whether the candidate is matched but not listed
  entry #incomplete   whether the shell should leave the word open after inserting it
  entry #aliases   other names listed beside the candidate, as flag aliases are
  entry #prefix   text before the core, inserted but not shown as part of the candidate
  entry #suffix   text after the core
  entry #expanded   whether the prefix and suffix have already been folded into the core
  entry #group   the `CommandGroup` a subcommand candidate belongs to
  entry #operand   whether this is a value for an operand, as opposed to a subcommand
      Both are offered identically at the cursor, but only a subcommand is part of the
      command's interface: without the distinction, `--help` would enumerate the working
      directory as though it were syntax.
  entry #display   what the menu shows when that differs from what is inserted
      Completing inside a clustered short flag needs it: choosing `-c` at `-ab` inserts
      only `c`, but the menu still names `-c`. Only zsh can show one thing and insert
      another.
  entry #text   the whole word: prefix, core and suffix

entry .internal   the macro implementation behind `Status.Admissible`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the object exists so that the macro has a stable home.
  entry .admissible   the macro behind `Status.Admissible.derived`: a union of status singletons to their values

entry .Help   the interface of a command as a tree: its flags, subcommands, statuses and variables, rendered as help text
  detail
      A `Help` node is one command: its `#command` name, `#description`, `#parameters`
      (each a `Help.Param`), `#subcommands` (each a `Help`), its `#group`, the exit
      `#statuses` its `execute` block can return and the environment `#variables` its
      prelude reads. `helpTree` builds the whole tree from an application by re-running its
      prelude in completion mode at every subcommand prefix, so nothing in it is written by
      hand; `Executive#help` is the executive's door to the same. `#teletype` renders a node
      in an aligned, man-page-style layout, and `.printable` lets a `Help` be passed
      straight to `Out.println`; the `roff` extension in `exoskeleton.manpage` renders the
      same tree as a manpage.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val help: Help =
        Help
          ( t"mytool",
            t"a build tool",
            List(Help.Param(t"--verbose", List(t"-v"), t"print detail", false, global = true)),
            List(Help(t"build", t"compile the project", Nil, Nil)) )

      help.teletype(80)
      help.local(List(t"build")).let(_.teletype(80))
      ```

      The rendering has a usage line (global options before `<command>`, local after, a
      single possible flag named outright), the description wrapped to the width, then
      _Global options_, _Options_ and _Commands_ sections — a leaf command shows plain
      _Options_ unless it is a local view carrying ancestors' flags — a section per
      `CommandGroup` with the group's common flags factored out, and _Exit statuses_
      gathered from the whole tree and deduplicated by code. A required flag is marked
      `(required)`; a repeatable one `...`; an operand `<name>`.
  refer exoskeleton.helpTree
  refer exoskeleton.roff
  refer exoskeleton.Manual
  refer exoskeleton.Status
  entry .Param   one flag of a command, as help shows it
    detail
        `Help.Param(name, aliases, description, repeatable, global, operand, required)`:
        the name as written (`--jobs`), its aliases likewise, the description, whether it
        repeats, whether it is global (checked before subcommand dispatch, so it precedes
        the subcommand on the command line), the operand's placeholder (`Unset` for a
        switch), and whether it is required.
    entry #name   the flag as written, `--name` or `-n`
    entry #aliases   the other names, as written
    entry #description   the help text
    entry #repeatable   whether the flag may be given more than once
    entry #global   whether the flag belongs before the subcommand
    entry #operand   the operand's placeholder, or `Unset` for a switch
    entry #required   whether the flag must be given
  entry .printable   lets a `Help` be printed with `Out.println`, wrapped to the terminal's width
      Renders with `#teletype` at the termcap's width and borrows escapade's `Teletype is
      Printable`. Needs a `Hyphenation`, which `Hyphenation.fallback` satisfies without an
      import, wrapping only at spaces; import a language's patterns (`hyphenations.english`)
      for hyphenated wrapping.
  entry #command   the command's name; for a `#local` view, the full path joined with spaces
  entry #description   the command's description, if any
  entry #parameters   the command's flags
  entry #subcommands   the nested commands
  entry #group   the `CommandGroup` this command is listed under, if any
  entry #statuses   the exit statuses the command's `execute` block can return; empty for a hand-built node
  entry #variables   the environment variables the command's prelude reads; empty for a hand-built node
  entry #local   the view of the tree from inside a subcommand path, or `Unset` if the path does not exist
      `help.local(List(t"distribution", t"ubuntu"))` selects that node, names it by the full
      path so the usage line reads `mytool distribution ubuntu`, and carries every
      ancestor's flags in as global options, with the node's own as local; the nearest root
      wins a name collision. `Cli#matches` is the path to pass for the subcommand matched
      so far.
  entry #teletype   renders the node as styled text, wrapped to a width
      Needs a `Hyphenation` for the wrapping. Below twenty columns the text overruns rather
      than wrapping.

entry .Completion   the `Cli` of a tab-completion request: records what the prelude asks for and renders it for the shell
  detail
      `executives.completionsExecutive` builds a `Completion` for a `{completions}`
      request, and `helpTree` builds one per probe. It extends `Cli` with the request's
      particulars — the `#shell`, which argument is being completed (`#currentArgument`),
      where the cursor is in it (`#focusPosition`), the `#tty` and the `#tab` count — and
      overrides the recording methods so that running the application's prelude fills its
      tables: `#flags` consulted, their `#operandNames`, the `#globalFlags` consulted
      before any subcommand was offered, the `#seenFlags` already on the line, the
      `#requiredFlags`, the `#statuses` an `execute` block admits, the `#explanation`, and
      the `#cursorSuggestions` offered for the focused argument. `#serialize` then renders
      the result as the lines the shell's script reads.

      `proceed` is always true: the prelude runs, and `execute` returns without running its
      block.
  refer exoskeleton.Cli
  refer exoskeleton.Completions
  refer exoskeleton.helpTree
  entry #fullArguments   the raw `{completions}` request, including the shell, cursor and tty words
  entry #arguments   the words of the command line being completed, after the command name
  entry #environment   the environment of the shell asking
  entry #workingDirectory   the directory of the shell asking
  entry #shell   which shell is asking, and so how `#serialize` formats its answer
  entry #currentArgument   the position of the argument being completed
  entry #focusPosition   the cursor's offset within that argument, or `Unset` when the shell gave none
  entry #stdio   the standard streams of the request
  entry #tty   the terminal the shell runs on, keying the tab-press count
  entry #tab   how many times tab has been pressed at this position
  entry #login   the user who ran the shell
  entry #flags   every flag the prelude consulted, with the `Discoverable` for its operand
  entry #operandNames   the operand placeholder of each flag which takes one
  entry #globalFlags   the flags consulted before any subcommand was offered, which apply regardless of the subcommand
  entry #seenFlags   the non-repeatable flags already on the command line, which are not offered again
  entry #requiredFlags   the flags demanded by `require()` or `validate()`
  entry #statuses   the exit statuses recorded by `execute` blocks, in order
  entry #explanation   the paragraph set by `explain`, shown as zsh's completion header
  entry #cursorSuggestions   the candidates offered for the focused argument
  entry #proceed   always `true`
  entry #parameter   reads a flag's operand through the interpreter
  entry #focused   whether an argument is the one being completed, taking its format into account
  entry #flagSuggestions   the flags not yet on the line, as suggestions; only long names when the word begins `--`
  entry #focusText   the whole text of the argument being completed, or empty
  entry #serialize   the suggestions as the lines the shell's completion script reads
      A word typed as a flag can only be extended by a flag, so other candidates are dropped
      there; when every candidate is hidden, the flags are offered too, or no shell would
      list anything. Candidates are wrapped by the focused word's format when the focus is
      that word. zsh receives `compadd` argument lists separated by NUL, with descriptions
      aligned and hidden candidates marked; bash receives bare words filtered by the typed
      prefix; fish and PowerShell receive tab-separated word and description lines, with a
      sole incomplete candidate given a space-terminated twin for fish.

entry .Completions   the shell completion scripts, their installation and the tab-press counter
  detail
      The script for each shell does nothing but forward the current command line, the
      cursor and the shell's name to the application as a `{completions}` request, so a
      new subcommand or flag never needs a script changed. `.script` is the text of one;
      `.install` finds where each shell loads completions from and writes it there, which
      the built-in `{admin} install` subcommand runs:

      ```sh
      mytool '{admin}' install
      ```

      `.ensure` is the fire-and-forget form for a daemon-backed tool to call at startup.
      Both need an `Entrypoint^`, an `Environment` and a `System` — the *invocation's*, since
      under a daemon the resident JVM's environment is whichever client's launcher started
      it — a `WorkingDirectory`, `Diagnostics` and a `CliEvent is Loggable`; a `Cli` in scope
      supplies the environment and directory, `systems.javaBaseSystem` the system.
  refer exoskeleton.Shell
  refer exoskeleton.Install
  refer exoskeleton.CliEvent
  entry .script   the completion script for a shell, forwarding the command line to `command '{completions}' …`
      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Completions.script(Shell.Zsh, t"mytool")
      ```

      zsh's is a compdef function reading `compadd` arguments; bash's uses
      `_init_completion` and `COMPREPLY`; fish's registers `complete -f -c`; PowerShell's
      registers a native argument completer and is appended to the profile.
  entry .install   writes the scripts into place for every shell found, or one script into the first writable directory
    detail
        `install(force)` needs `Effectful`, so it cannot run from the prelude that
        completion runs. It checks that the command named by the entry point's script is
        the running executable (`Installation.CommandNotOnPath` otherwise, unless `force`),
        then asks each installed shell, all at once and with five seconds in all, where it
        loads completions from — with standard input closed and standard error discarded,
        so a startup file which prompts or complains neither holds it up nor corrupts the
        answer, and with the invocation's `HOME`, `ZDOTDIR`, `XDG_CONFIG_HOME`,
        `XDG_DATA_HOME`, `XDG_DATA_DIRS` and `BASH_COMPLETION_USER_DIR` passed on. zsh:
        the first existing writable `$fpath` directory under the home, else the first
        writable one, else `$XDG_DATA_HOME/zsh/site-functions`, created, with result
        `Unconfirmed`. bash: `${BASH_COMPLETION_USER_DIR:-$XDG_DATA_HOME/bash-completion}/completions`,
        `Unconfirmed` unless an interactive bash defines bash-completion's loader. fish: the
        `completions` directory of its configuration directory. PowerShell: appended to the
        profile, once. Raises `Install.Error` for a failure to query the environment or to
        write.

        `install(shell, command, scriptName, dirs)` writes one shell's script into the first
        of `dirs` which exists and is writable, as `Installed`, `AlreadyInstalled` or
        `NoWritableLocation`.
  entry .ensure   installs the scripts once per JVM, quietly, or with `force` reinstalls and reports the paths
      Without `force`, the install runs at most once per JVM lifetime (the shell queries
      cost several hundred milliseconds, which would dominate a daemon-backed tool's launch
      if repeated) and nothing is returned; with `force`, the scripts are reinstalled and
      the paths written are returned, which is what `{admin} install` prints. Errors are
      swallowed.
  entry .tab   the number of times tab has been pressed at one position, counted per terminal
      `Completions.tab(tty, Tab(arguments, focus, cursor))` returns `Prim` for a first
      press and increments while the same line is completed at the same place on the same
      tty; a different request resets it. The count reaches `Discoverable#discover` as
      `tab`, so a second press can reveal hidden files.
  entry .Tab   the key of a tab-press count: the arguments, the focused word, the cursor and the count so far
    detail
        Two presses are the same request when their `Tab`s agree with the count zeroed.
    entry #arguments   the request's arguments
    entry #focus   the focused argument's position
    entry #cursor   the cursor's offset
    entry #count   how many presses so far
    entry #next   the same key with the count incremented
    entry #zero   the same key with the count zeroed, for comparison
  entry .Installation   the result of installing the scripts: not on the path, or one result per shell
    detail
        An enum: `CommandNotOnPath(script)` when the command the scripts would complete is
        not the running executable, and `Shells(zsh, bash, fish, powershell)` with an
        `InstallResult` for each. Its `Communicable` renders either as a message; `#paths`
        lists the files written.
    entry .communicable   renders the result as a message, one paragraph per shell
    entry .InstallResult   what happened for one shell
      detail
          `Installed(shell, path)`, `AlreadyInstalled(shell, path)`,
          `NoWritableLocation(shell)`, `ShellNotInstalled(shell)`, or `Unconfirmed(shell,
          path)`: the script was written, but the shell could not be shown to load it from
          there — the directory is not one it searches, it lacks bash-completion, or it did
          not answer in time. An exhaustive match must handle all five.
      entry .communicable   renders the result as a sentence naming the shell and path
      entry #pathname   the path written, for `Installed`, `AlreadyInstalled` and `Unconfirmed`; else `Unset`
    entry #paths   every path written, across the shells

entry .CliEvent   what completions installation logs: subprocess events and the locations written
  detail
      An enum: `Exec(event)` wraps a guillotine `Exec.Event` from the shells queried, and
      `Installing(location)` names a file written. `Completions.install` and `ensure` need a
      `CliEvent is Loggable` in scope; `logging.silentLogging` discards them.
  entry .execEvent   transcribes a guillotine `Exec.Event` as a `CliEvent.Exec`
  entry .communicable   renders each event as a message

entry .execute   the block of an application which does the work, run only on a real invocation
  detail
      Under `executives.completionsExecutive` the body must return an `Execution`, and the
      only way to make one is an `execute` block. The code before it — the prelude — runs
      both when the command is invoked and when completions or help are requested; the
      block runs only on a real invocation, with an `Effectful` and the `Invocation` in
      scope, so it is where `Prospective` and `Requisite` handles are applied and where
      output is written.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import backstops.genericErrorMessageBackstop
      import executives.completionsExecutive
      import interpreters.posixInterpreter
      import systems.javaBaseSystem

      val Build = Subcommand(t"build", e"compile the project")

      def mytool(args: List[Text]): Unit = application(args):
        arguments match
          case Build() :: _ =>
            val jobs = Flag[Int](t"jobs", aliases = List('j'))()
            execute:
              Out.println(t"building with ${jobs().or(1)} jobs")
              Exit.Ok

          case _ =>
            execute(Exit.Fail(1))
      ```

      Before running the block, `execute` checks what the prelude accrued on the
      `Invocation`: if any `require()`d flag is missing or any `validate()`d operand is
      malformed, it prints them all to standard error — _The following required options
      were not specified_, _The following options were given invalid values_ — and returns
      `Exit.Fail(2)` without running the block, which is why a `Requisite` unwraps
      unconditionally inside it.

      The block returns any `Termination` — an `Exit`, a `Status` object, or a union of
      them — and its result type is kept precise, so `Status.Admissible` can reify the
      statuses it may return; they are recorded on the `Cli` even in completion mode, where
      the block is not run, which is how help documents them. The result is `Execution to
      result`.
  refer exoskeleton.Execution
  refer exoskeleton.Status
  refer exoskeleton.Effectful
  refer exoskeleton.Invocation

entry .Execution   the result of an application body under the completions executive: an exit status and the admissible statuses
  detail
      Only `execute` makes one. `#exitStatus` is what the process exits with and
      `#statuses` are the `Status` values the block could have returned; `#Result` refines
      the type to the union of terminations the block returns, so `Execution to Nothing` is
      a block which never returns normally.
  entry .of   makes an `Execution to result` from an exit status and the statuses
  entry #exitStatus   the exit status
  entry #statuses   the statuses the block could return
  entry #Result   the union of terminations the block returns

entry .explain   attaches a paragraph of help to the current subcommand section
    `explain(t"…")` records an explanation on the `Cli` in scope, shown as the header of
    zsh's completion list; the function receives the prior explanation as the context value
    `prior`, so it may extend rather than replace. Written in the prelude of a deep
    subcommand, it documents that subcommand where it is defined. No-op outside a
    `Completion`.

entry .helpTree   builds a `Help` tree for an application by re-running its prelude at every subcommand prefix
  detail
      `helpTree(command, environment, workingDirectory, stdio, login)(block)` runs the
      application's body as a zsh `Completion` with the cursor at the end of an empty final
      word, first at the empty prefix and then at each subcommand suggested, descending
      into every candidate which is not hidden, incomplete or an operand value. Each run
      yields a `Probe` — the suggestions, flags, globals, required flags, operand names,
      statuses and variables seen — and the tree is assembled from them: a flag already
      attributed to an ancestor is dropped from deeper nodes, suggestions are deduplicated
      by core, and children are ordered by name. The environment is wrapped in a recording
      view, so every variable the prelude reads is listed in the node's `variables`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import interpreters.posixInterpreter
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory
      import stdios.muteStdio

      val Build = Subcommand(t"build", e"compile the project")

      def app(using Cli): Execution =
        Flag(t"verbose", description = t"verbose output")()
        arguments match
          case Build() :: _ =>
            Flag[Int](t"jobs", description = t"parallel jobs")()
            execute(Exit.Ok)

          case _ =>
            execute(Exit.Fail(1))

      val tree: Help =
        helpTree(t"mytool", summon[Environment], summon[WorkingDirectory], summon[Stdio], Login(t"me", Unset))(app)

      tree.subcommands.map(_.command)
      tree.parameters.map(_.name)
      ```

      Needs an `Interpreter`. `execute` does nothing in completion mode, so the block runs
      harmlessly; but a prelude with effects of its own runs them once per probe.
  refer exoskeleton.Help
  refer exoskeleton.Probe
  refer exoskeleton.Completion

entry .Probe   what one run of an application's prelude at one argument prefix reveals about its interface
  detail
      The record `helpTree` takes from each `Completion` it drives: the `#suggestions` at
      the cursor (the subcommands to descend into), the `#flags` consulted, which of them
      are `#globals` and `#required`, their `#operands`' placeholders, the `#statuses`
      recorded and the environment `#variables` read.
  entry #suggestions   the candidates offered for the final, empty word
  entry #flags   every flag consulted
  entry #globals   the flags consulted before any subcommand was offered
  entry #required   the flags demanded
  entry #operands   each flag's operand placeholder
  entry #statuses   the statuses recorded by `execute` blocks reached
  entry #variables   the environment variables read

entry .Pathname   a positional argument completed as a filesystem path, and the extractor which resolves it
  detail
      `case Pathname(path) :: _ =>` matches any argument, offering the paths its text could
      begin as suggestions, and binds the argument resolved against the working directory
      as a `Path on Local`. Completion descends a directory at a time (a directory candidate
      is `incomplete`, so the shell leaves the word open), hides dotfiles until tab is
      pressed a second time or the typed name begins with `.`, expands a leading `~` as the
      shell would — a quoted argument and every partial word reach the program with the
      tilde intact — and abbreviates the results back under it. Every candidate is an
      operand value, so the help tree never enumerates the working directory.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import interpreters.posixInterpreter
      import systems.javaBaseSystem
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

      def verify(args: List[Argument])(using Cli): Optional[Path on Local] = args match
        case Pathname(file) :: _ => file
        case _                   => Unset
      ```

      Needs a `WorkingDirectory`, a `Cli` and a `System` (for the home directory). For a
      flag's operand, `pathnameDiscoverable` does the same.
  refer exoskeleton.pathnameDiscoverable
  refer exoskeleton.Discoverable
  entry .complete   the path candidates for a partially-typed operand
      `Pathname.complete(operand, tab)` is the completion the extractor and
      `pathnameDiscoverable` share: `~`-expanded, resolved against the working directory,
      descending into a directory as it is typed, with hidden entries shown on a repeated
      tab. Empty when the operand cannot be resolved.
  entry .unapply   offers path completions for an argument and resolves it, as a Scala `Option`
      Adds its candidates to whatever another extractor evaluated against the same
      argument — typically a `Subcommand` — already offered.

entry .pathnameDiscoverable   completes a `Flag[Path on Local]`'s operand as a filesystem path
    The `Discoverable` for `Path on Local`, so that `--file <TAB>` completes a path exactly
    as a positional `Pathname` argument does. The subject belongs to serpentine and
    `Discoverable`'s companion cannot see galilei, so the given is structurally
    un-anchorable: it lives at the top level and must be imported by name. Needs a
    `WorkingDirectory` and a `System`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import soundness.pathnameDiscoverable
    import interpreters.posixInterpreter
    import systems.javaBaseSystem
    import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

    def file(using Cli): Boolean = Flag[Path on Local](t"file").present
    ```

entry .Manual   the static metadata a manpage needs beyond what the `Help` tree discovers
  detail
      Declared once, as a `given Manual` or passed to `roff`; every field is optional, so
      an application without one still renders a complete, if spartan, page. Per-command
      and per-flag descriptions do not belong here — they stay on the `Subcommand`, `Flag`
      and `CommandGroup` declarations the tree already carries. A `Manual` adds what
      discovery cannot supply: what an environment variable means, an example invocation,
      the authors, the files, the bugs, and the page's section, version and date.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val manual = Manual
        ( prose       = t"mytool builds the project.",
          examples    = List(Manual.Example(t"build with four jobs", t"mytool build --jobs 4")),
          environment = List(Manual.EnvironmentVariable(t"MYTOOL_HOME", t"the home directory")),
          seeAlso     = List(Manual.Reference(t"make")) )
      ```

      A status declared in `exitStatuses` and one discovered from an `execute` block may
      share a code; the hand-written description wins. A variable described here and one
      discovered are merged by name.
  refer exoskeleton.roff
  refer exoskeleton.Help
  entry .Section   the manual section the page belongs to: user commands, system calls, …
    detail
        An enum of the eight standard sections, `UserCommands` through
        `SystemAdministration`, each knowing its `#number` (1 to 8) and `#title`.
    entry #number   the section's number, from 1 for `UserCommands`
    entry #title   the section's heading, such as `User Commands`
  entry .Reference   a `SEE ALSO` entry: a page name and its section, 1 by default
    entry #name   the page referred to
    entry #section   its section number
  entry .ExitStatus   a hand-written exit status: a code and its description
    entry #code   the exit code
    entry #description   what it means
  entry .EnvironmentVariable   a described environment variable, for the `ENVIRONMENT` section
    entry #name   the variable's name
    entry #description   what it controls
  entry .ManFile   a file the program uses, for the `FILES` section
    entry #path   the file's path, shown in italics
    entry #description   what it holds
  entry .Example   an example invocation, with an optional caption
    entry #caption   a sentence introducing the example, if any
    entry #command   the command line shown
  entry #synopsisName   the one-line summary after the name; the tree's description by default
  entry #section   the manual section; `UserCommands` by default
  entry #version   the program's version, shown in the page footer as `command version`
  entry #date   the page's date, shown ISO-8601
  entry #prose   a paragraph for the `DESCRIPTION` section, after the tree's description
  entry #authors   one paragraph each in `AUTHORS`
  entry #examples   the `EXAMPLES` section
  entry #exitStatuses   hand-written statuses, merged with the discovered ones by code
  entry #environment   described variables, merged with the discovered ones by name
  entry #files   the `FILES` section
  entry #seeAlso   the `SEE ALSO` references
  entry #bugs   the `BUGS` section
  entry #homepage   a URL, shown as `Homepage: …` after the references

entry .roff   renders a `Help` tree as a manpage, with a `Manual`'s metadata
  detail
      An extension on `Help`: `help.roff` (or `help.roff(using manual)`, defaulting to an
      empty `Manual`) is a virility `Roff` page, whose `serialize` is the troff text. The
      structure — NAME, SYNOPSIS, DESCRIPTION, OPTIONS (GLOBAL OPTIONS and OPTIONS for a
      tool with subcommands), COMMANDS with a subsection per `CommandGroup`, EXAMPLES, EXIT
      STATUS, ENVIRONMENT, FILES, AUTHORS, BUGS, SEE ALSO — comes from the tree and the
      manual, each section present only when it has content, so `--help` and `man` cannot
      disagree. Statuses and variables are gathered from every command in the tree. Styling
      in descriptions is stripped.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val help: Help = Help(t"mytool", t"a build tool", Nil, List(Help(t"build", t"compile", Nil, Nil)))
      help.roff(using Manual(authors = List(t"William Shakespeare"))).serialize
      ```

      `Manpages.install` writes the page into the XDG man hierarchy.
  refer exoskeleton.Manual
  refer exoskeleton.Manpages

entry .Manpages   installs a rendered manpage into the XDG man hierarchy
  detail
      `Manpages.install(page)` writes a `Roff` page to
      `$XDG_DATA_HOME/man/man<section>/<title>.<section>`, creating the directories; that
      directory is on the default manpath of modern man-db, so no `MANPATH` change is
      needed. The environment and system are the *invocation's* (a `Cli` in scope supplies
      the `Environment`; `systems.javaBaseSystem` the `System`), since under a daemon the
      JVM's own environment is whichever client's launcher started it.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import systems.javaBaseSystem
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

      val help: Help = Help(t"mytool", t"a build tool", Nil, Nil)
      effectful(Manpages.install(help.roff)).pathname
      ```
  refer exoskeleton.roff
  refer exoskeleton.Install
  entry .install   writes the page, or reports that one is already there; `force` overwrites it
      Needs `Effectful`, an `Environment`, a `System`, `Diagnostics` and an `Io.Event is
      Loggable`; raises `Install.Error` with `Reason.Io` for a failure to write, and reports
      `NoWritableLocation` when the XDG directory cannot be resolved.
  entry .InstallResult   `Installed(path)`, `AlreadyInstalled(path)` or `NoWritableLocation`
    detail
        An enum with a `Communicable` rendering each as a sentence.
    entry .communicable   renders the result as a sentence
    entry #pathname   the path of the page, for `Installed` and `AlreadyInstalled`; else `Unset`

entry .Enclave   the test rig: packages an application as a daemon-backed executable and runs it in a sandbox
  detail
      An `Enclave` is a superlunary `Rig` for command-line applications. `Enclave(name)
      .dispatch { '{ … } }` compiles the quoted application body against the classloader in
      scope, stages it as a JAR, packages the JAR with the `xek` builder — found in `$XEK`,
      else `dist/xek` under the working directory — into a launcher executable named
      `name`, and yields an `Enclave.Launcher`. `Launcher#sandbox` then starts the tool's
      daemon under a scratch home and hands a `Tool` to a block, in which `shell.pane()`
      opens a real shell with the tool's completion script loaded, so a test can press tab
      and read what appears. The quoted body is an ethereal daemon body, so it
      wraps the application in `cli:`, and the tool it builds answers each invocation and
      completion request from one resident process. The daemon is killed and the scratch home deleted on every exit
      from the block.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import systems.javaBaseSystem
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import logging.silentLogging
      import threads.platformThreads

      given classloader: Classloader = Classloader[Option[Int]]

      val Build = Subcommand(t"build", e"compile the project")

      supervise:
        Enclave(t"mytool").dispatch:
          ' {
              import executives.completionsExecutive
              import interpreters.posixInterpreter

              cli:
                arguments match
                  case Build() :: _ => execute(Exit.Ok)
                  case _            => execute(Exit.Fail(1))

              t"finished"
            }

        . sandbox:
            Shell.Zsh.pane()(Pane.completions(t""))
      ```

      The optional `buildId`, `releaseKey`, `recoveryKey` and `appId` are baked into the
      executable's record so that its launcher accepts a signed upgrade; without a release
      key and an application id it refuses every one. `Enclave.keygen` makes a key pair and
      `Launcher#sign` signs a build with it (Java 24 or later). It needs a `Classloader` and
      an `Environment` when constructed, and an `Enclave` is typed `Enclave^{parasite.threads}`.

      This is `exoskeleton.rig`, a test dependency: it needs the `xek` builder, the shells
      under test, and optionally tmux, on the host.
  refer exoskeleton.Pane
  refer exoskeleton.pane
  entry .Error   error 347: the sandboxed tool's daemon did not report a process ID
    detail
        Raised by `Launcher#sandbox` when `tool '{admin}' pid` prints nothing. Overwhelmingly
        the cause is a protocol mismatch: the launcher the tool was packaged with and the
        daemon it starts carry different launcher schema signatures, so the daemon refuses
        the first document and closes the connection — a stale `xek` builder, in practice.
    entry #tool   the path of the tool which did not answer
  entry .Tool   a built, running tool: its path, its daemon's pid and the scratch home it runs under
    detail
        A shared capability, since every pane of a suite drives the one tool; its lifetime
        is the `sandbox` block.
    entry #path   the executable's path
    entry #pid   the daemon's process id
    entry #home   the scratch home its daemon and its panes' shells run under
    entry #command   the tool's name, as typed at a prompt
    entry #completions   runs a block while `{admin} await` listens, and returns what the tool logged
        Needs a `Monitor` and an `Environment`. `Cli.log` messages made during the block are
        returned joined, or `failed`.
  entry .Launcher   the packaged executable, to sign or to run in a sandbox
    detail
        What `Enclave#dispatch` yields.
    entry #path   the executable's path
    entry #sign   signs the executable with a key seed, writing the signed copy to `out`
        Runs `xek sign`; `foreign = true` allows a key the executable's record does not
        carry, which a launcher will then reject. Raises `Exec.Error`.
    entry #sandbox   starts the tool's daemon under a scratch home, runs a block with the `Tool`, then kills it
        Needs an `Environment` and tactics for `Enclave.Error`, `Exec.Error`,
        `Number.Error` and `Path.Error`. The scratch home replaces `HOME` and the XDG
        directories (`Enclave.scratch`), so nothing of the real user's configuration is
        read or written; the tool is not installed — each pane writes the completion script
        it needs into the scratch home itself. The daemon is killed and the home deleted in
        a `finally`, since an abandoned daemon holds a JVM for the rest of the session.
  entry .scratch   the variables which direct a user's configuration into a scratch home
      `HOME`, `XDG_CONFIG_HOME`, `XDG_DATA_HOME` and `BASH_COMPLETION_USER_DIR`, all under
      the given home.
  entry .environment   an environment with the scratch variables over a base, or the JVM's with `XEK` naming a builder
      `Enclave.environment(base, home)` overlays `scratch(home)` on `base`.
      `Enclave.environment(builder)` is the JVM's environment with `XEK` set, for a suite
      which knows where its builder is but runs inside a daemon whose environment is
      sanitized and whose working directory is `/`.
  entry .keygen   generates an ML-DSA-44 key pair with `xek keygen`, as `<prefix>.seed` and `<prefix>.pub`
      Returns the seed path, to sign with, and the public key path, to build an `Enclave`
      with as its `releaseKey`. Raises `Exec.Error` and `Path.Error`; needs Java 24 or later.
  entry #name   the executable's name
  entry #buildId   a build identifier for the record, if any
  entry #releaseKey   the public key a signed upgrade must carry, if any
  entry #recoveryKey   the recovery key, if any
  entry #appId   the application id for the record, if any
  entry #Result   `Enclave.Launcher`
  entry #Form   `Text`
  entry #Target   `Path on Linux`
  entry #Transport   `Json`
  entry #stage   stages the JAR under `out/name` and packages it with `xek build`
      Raises `Rig.Error` with `Reason.Staging` for a naming, linking, async or execution
      failure; a packaging failure panics with xek's exit status.

entry .Pane   a terminal pane running a shell for a test: keys are typed at it and its screen is read
  detail
      A `Pane` is a capability — exclusive, since one test drives it at a time — whose
      lifetime is the block that creates it: `shell.pane()` runs the shell on a
      pseudo-terminal rendered by yossarian, and `shell.tmux()` runs it in a tmux session,
      an independent emulator against which yossarian's rendering is compared. The
      companion holds the test vocabulary: `Pane.enter` types keys, `Pane.attend` types
      them and waits for the screen to settle, `Pane.screenshot` reads the screen, and
      `Pane.completions` and `Pane.progress` ask the shell to complete a line for the
      tool under test.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import systems.javaBaseSystem
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import logging.silentLogging
      import threads.platformThreads

      def check(using Enclave.Tool, Monitor): Text =
        Shell.Bash.pane()(Pane.completions(t"build "))
      ```
  refer exoskeleton.pane
  refer exoskeleton.tmux
  refer exoskeleton.Enclave
  refer exoskeleton.Screenshot
  entry .keys   the key names a test may type, as tmux names them, and the bytes each sends
      `Left`, `Right`, `Up`, `Down`, `BSpace`, `Enter`, `Tab`, `Escape`, `C-m`, `C-l` and
      `C-c`; any other text is typed literally.
  entry .enter   types keypresses at the pane in scope: a key name from `.keys`, a character, or literal text
  entry .resize   resizes the pane in scope
  entry .screenshot   the screen of the pane in scope
  entry .waitFor   whether some line of the screen satisfies a predicate within a timeout, twenty seconds by default
      Polls every ten milliseconds. Needs a `Monitor`.
  entry .attend   types keypresses, then waits for the screen to change and then to settle
      A shell completing a line asks the tool through its daemon and may draw what it hears
      in several steps, so the screen is read once it has looked the same three times
      running, twenty milliseconds apart; if it does not change at all within five seconds
      the keys are taken to have changed nothing.
  entry .settle   waits until the screen has looked the same three times running, or five seconds have passed
  entry .completions   types the tool's name and a line, presses tab, and returns what the shell listed
      `Pane.completions(t"distribution ")` with a bash pane and the test's tool returns the
      candidates bash printed, for instance `gentoo   red hat  ubuntu`; zsh and fish
      include descriptions as they show them. Under PowerShell the rig's `_completions`
      function is called instead, since PowerShell's menu cannot be read from the screen.
      The prompt line is excluded.
  entry .progress   types the tool's name and a line, presses tab, and returns the command line as the shell left it
      The cursor's position is decorated, `^` by default, so `distribution ubuntu --^` shows
      that tab inserted the common prefix `--` of the flags and left the cursor after it.
  entry .boundary   the length of the longest prefix of some bytes which ends on a UTF-8 character boundary
      Used by `Display#render` to hold a character split between output chunks until the
      rest arrives.
  entry .Display   what a pseudo-terminal shows, rendered by yossarian as its output arrives
    detail
        A durable, thread-safe display: the tasks which read the shell's output and write
        yossarian's answers to its queries (cursor position, attributes) retain it. A
        sequence yossarian cannot render is kept and reported by the next `#screenshot`
        rather than lost.
    entry #width   the columns
    entry #height   the rows
    entry #send   writes bytes to the terminal, one writer at a time
    entry #resize   resets the display to a new size
    entry #screenshot   the screen, with trailing spaces trimmed, and the cursor; raises `Pane.Error` for an unrendered sequence
    entry #render   renders a chunk of the terminal's output, holding an incomplete trailing character
    entry #answer   writes yossarian's replies to the shell's queries back to it, until `#finish`
    entry #finish   stops the replies
  entry .Emulated   a pane on a pseudo-terminal, showing what a `Display` renders of it
    detail
        What `shell.pane()` hands its block: keys go to the pseudo-terminal's job, and the
        screen comes from the display.
    entry #shell   the shell running in the pane
    entry #width   the columns
    entry #height   the rows
    entry #screenshot   the display's screen
    entry #press   types a key name or literal text
    entry #resize   resizes the display and the pseudo-terminal
  entry .Error   error 271: the terminal could not be driven
    detail
        _can't drive the terminal: `reason`_. The reasons:

        ```tabular
        column  Case
        column  Code
        column  Message
        column  Note
        row  `ShellNotInstalled(shell)`  1  _the shell binary `shell` is not installed_
        row  `SessionDied`  2  _the terminal session terminated unexpectedly_
        row  `ExecFailed`  3  _could not start the terminal session_
        row  `Unrendered(problem)`  4  _the terminal could not render its output: `problem`_  yossarian could not render a sequence
        row  `NotReady(shell)`  5  _the shell `shell` did not become ready_  the shell did not show a prompt and echo the rig's marker in time
        row  `Unconfigured(problem)`  6  _the shell could not be configured: `problem`_  a configuration file could not be written into the scratch home
        ```
    entry .Reason   why the terminal could not be driven
      detail
          An enum with a `number` (1 to 6) and a `Communicable` given.
      entry #number   the subcode
    entry .communicable   renders a reason as its message
    entry #reason   the reason
  entry #shell   the shell running in the pane
  entry #width   the columns
  entry #height   the rows
  entry #press   types a key name from `Pane.keys`, or literal text
  entry #screenshot   the screen now
  entry #resize   resizes the pane, delivering `SIGWINCH` to the shell

entry .Screenshot   the screen of a pane: its lines, its size and the cursor
  detail
      `#screen` is one `Text` per row with trailing spaces trimmed, `#size` is `(columns,
      rows)` and `#cursor` is `(column, row)` as ordinals. `apply()` joins the rows with
      newlines.
  entry #screen   the rows
  entry #size   the columns and rows
  entry #cursor   the cursor's column and row
  entry #apply   the screen as one text, rows joined by newlines
  entry #currentLine   the row the cursor is on, trimmed, with the character under the cursor decorated
      `screenshot.currentLine(_ => t"^")` is what `Pane.progress` reads.

entry .Tmux   a pane in a tmux session, an independent terminal emulator
  detail
      What `shell.tmux()` hands its block. Keys are sent with `tmux send-keys`, which
      interprets tmux's own key names, and the screen is read with `tmux capture-pane`;
      each is a subprocess, so this backend is slow, and it exists to check yossarian's
      rendering against another emulator's. Also `Findable`.
  refer exoskeleton.tmux
  entry #id   the session's name, a UUID
  entry #workingDirectory   the directory tmux is run from
  entry #environment   the environment tmux's commands run with
  entry #width   the columns
  entry #height   the rows
  entry #shell   the shell running in the session
  entry #press   sends keys with `tmux send-keys`
  entry #resize   resizes the window with `tmux resize-window`, delivering `SIGWINCH`
  entry #screenshot   captures the pane and reads the cursor with `tmux display-message`

entry .pane   runs a shell on a pseudo-terminal with the tool under test configured, and hands the pane to a block
  detail
      An extension on `Shell`: `Shell.Zsh.pane(width = 80, height = 24)(action)` starts
      the shell as `rig.launch` configures it — under the tool's scratch home, with the
      tool's directory first on the `PATH`, a prompt of `> `, and the tool's completion
      script loaded in place of any configuration the user has — on a pseudo-terminal
      whose output yossarian renders into a `Pane.Display`, waits for the prompt, clears
      the screen, and runs the block with a `Pane.Emulated`. Needs a `WorkingDirectory`, an
      `Environment`, an `Enclave.Tool`, a `Monitor`, a `Tactic[Pane.Error]` and a guillotine
      `Exec.Event is Loggable`.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import systems.javaBaseSystem
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import logging.silentLogging
      import threads.platformThreads

      def flags(using Enclave.Tool, Monitor): Text =
        Shell.Fish.pane(width = 120)(Pane.completions(t"build "))
      ```
  refer exoskeleton.Pane
  refer exoskeleton.tmux
  refer exoskeleton.rig

entry .tmux   runs a shell in a tmux session, configured exactly as `pane` does, and hands the pane to a block
  detail
      An extension on `Shell`, with the same parameters and contextual needs as `pane`. The
      session is created detached at the given size, named by a UUID, and killed when the
      block ends. Requires tmux on the host; a missing binary surfaces as `Pane.Error` with
      `ExecFailed`.
  refer exoskeleton.pane
  refer exoskeleton.Tmux

entry .rig   how the rig starts and readies a shell for a test
  detail
      The pieces `pane` and `tmux` share: `#launch` builds the command which starts a
      shell under the tool's scratch home with the rig's own configuration, `#ready` waits
      for it, and `#powershell` is the PowerShell profile. Exposed for a suite which drives
      a shell some other way.
  entry .launch   the command which starts a shell for a test, configured for the tool under test
      Writes a configuration of the rig's own into the scratch home — a bash rcfile, a
      zsh startup file with the completion function on `fpath`, a fish `config.fish`, or a
      PowerShell profile — each giving a prompt of `> ` and loading the tool's completion
      script as `Completions.script` writes it; sets `PATH` to put the tool first,
      `POWERSHELL_UPDATECHECK` off, and `TERM` to `xterm-256color` when `terminal` is
      true (tmux sets its own). Raises `Pane.Error` with `Unconfigured` if a file cannot
      be written.
  entry .directory   the directory part of a path's text
  entry .ready   waits until the shell has started and loaded its configuration, then clears the screen
      Waits for a `>` prompt, then (except under PowerShell) asks the shell to echo a short
      random marker and waits for it, so every line of the configuration is known to have
      run, and presses `C-l`. Raises `Pane.Error` with `NotReady` after twenty seconds.
  entry .powershell   the PowerShell profile the rig uses: a `> ` prompt, tab completion through the tool, and `_completions`
      `_completions "line"` prints each completion of a line as `name@@description`, which
      is how `Pane.completions` reads PowerShell's candidates, since its menu is not
      written to the screen.
